    answer: "A structural observation report must be signed by the engineer of record (EOR) for the structure, or by a licensed professional engineer who has taken responsible charge of the structural observation itself. No one else's signature counts: not the contractor, not the special inspector, not a junior engineer acting alone. The reason is what the signature certifies. A structural observation report is a professional engineering judgment — that the structural work observed generally conforms to the design concept in the sealed drawings. Only a PE with responsible charge of that judgment can stand behind it.\\n\\nResponsible charge is the key legal concept. The signing PE must have exercised responsible charge over the observation: directing what gets observed and when, reviewing the field findings, and rendering the conformance assessment personally. A field representative can gather the observations — photographs, measurements, notes — but the report's conclusions belong to the PE who supervised that representative directly and reviewed the findings. When I sign an observation report, I'm certifying that I controlled the engineering decisions behind every statement in it, including the decision that a deviation is acceptable or requires correction.\\n\\nThe signature also draws the line between the two field roles owners confuse. The special inspector signs inspection reports as factual records — this weld was inspected, these bolts were verified — under their agency's program. The EOR signs observation reports as engineering conclusions — the work observed conforms to my design intent, or it doesn't, and here's the required disposition. At closeout, the building official checks both streams: the inspector's factual record and the EOR's professional judgment. A report signed by someone without responsible charge satisfies neither, and most plan reviewers will reject it and ask for the EOR's signature before final inspection.",
