    h1: "Does an Industrial Mezzanine Need a PE Stamp?",
    answer: "An industrial mezzanine needs a PE stamp in virtually every jurisdiction — it's a new floor structure inside your building, and AHJs treat it as engineered construction. The mezzanine adds gravity loads to the columns and footings, creates a new occupied level with egress requirements, and in seismic country adds mass whose earthquake forces must be anchored and braced. Prefabricated mezzanine systems come with manufacturer engineering, but the PE still has to verify that engineering against the site's code, the actual storage loads, and the building it's installed in. The manufacturer's catalog doesn't know your seismic zone or your slab.\n\nWhat I verify starts with the loads. Storage loads are the governing question: the weight per square foot the operation will actually place on the deck, including the worst credible arrangement — fully loaded pallet positions, concentrated equipment, the forklift or pallet jack if it goes up there. I design or verify the deck, beams, and columns for those loads plus the code's requirements, then trace the column loads down: can the existing slab carry the new point loads, or does the mezzanine need its own footings? That slab check is the most common surprise — warehouse slabs designed for uniform floor loading often can't take mezzanine column point loads without thickened footings or new foundations.\n\nEgress and fire protection complete the review. A mezzanine with people on it needs code-compliant egress: stair capacity, travel distances, and the number of exits for the occupant load. The space beneath and the mezzanine level itself need sprinkler and fire protection coordination — storage under a mezzanine changes the fire hazard picture. In California and Nevada, the seismic design — bracing, anchorage to the building, and the interaction with the building's own lateral system — gets the full engineering treatment. When loads, supports, egress, and seismic all verify, the PE seals.",
