    h1: "Does a Grease Interceptor Need PE Certification?",
    answer: "A grease interceptor needs engineered sizing — and often a PE's certification — whenever the AHJ or the sewer utility requires it, which is standard for restaurants, commercial kitchens, and food processing. The interceptor isn't just a box in the ground; it's sized from the fixture load, the wastewater flow, and the local pretreatment requirements, and the plumbing permit's grease waste design has to show that sizing. Many utilities run their own fats, oils, and grease (FOG) programs with interceptor sizing formulas and approved device lists that the design must satisfy before the sewer connection is approved.\n\nWhat I do is size the interceptor from the plumbing load: the fixtures discharging grease waste — sinks, dishwashers, floor drains in food prep areas — converted to flow rates, then matched to the interceptor capacity the code and the utility require. The location gets designed too: interceptors need accessibility for pumping and maintenance, proper venting, and the inlet and outlet piping configured so the device actually separates grease instead of passing it through. An undersized or inaccessible interceptor fails its purpose and fails inspection; both are expensive to fix after the slab is poured.\n\nHealth department and utility coordination runs alongside the plumbing design. The health reviewer checks the grease waste plumbing against food code expectations, while the utility's pretreatment program may dictate the interceptor type — gravity or hydromechanical — the minimum size, and the maintenance reporting the owner signs up for. I design to the strictest of the overlapping requirements — code, utility program, health expectations — and the sealed plumbing drawings document the sizing basis so every reviewer sees the same engineering. The PE's certification, where required, states the interceptor is sized for the connected load per the applicable requirements.",
