    h1: "Do ADA Upgrades Need a PE Stamp?",
    answer: "ADA upgrades need a PE stamp wherever the accessibility work involves structural or MEP engineering — new ramps with structural framing, restroom reconfigurations with relocated plumbing, widened openings in bearing walls, or regraded accessible routes with retaining and drainage. Purely architectural accessibility scope — signage, hardware, striping — doesn't need engineering. But the moment the upgrade cuts structure, moves plumbing, or regrades the site, the PE's review and seal apply to that engineered scope. The engineering answers how to build it; it doesn't answer the legal question of what the law requires.\n\nThat boundary matters and I state it plainly: I am an engineer, not an attorney, and my review covers the construction scope, not legal compliance advice. The accessibility requirements themselves — which elements, what dimensions, what triggers the upgrade — come from the architect's code analysis, the CASp inspector's report in California, or legal counsel's assessment. My PE scope starts where that analysis says structural or MEP work is needed: designing the ramp structure, engineering the restroom's new plumbing and structural modifications, verifying the regraded route's drainage and retaining.\n\nWhat I verify in the engineered scope is standard review-and-seal work applied to accessibility construction. Ramp structures get designed for loads, slip resistance detailing, edge protection, and drainage — a ramp that ponds water is a hazard the engineering should prevent. Restroom reconfigurations get the plumbing isometric review, the structural review of any wall or floor modifications, and fixture layout coordination. Site regrading gets the drainage design so the accessible route doesn't become the site's low point. The PE seals the engineered construction; the accessibility compliance determination stays with the qualified design professional or inspector who made it.",
    directAnswer: "ADA upgrades need a PE stamp for the structural and MEP portions — ramps, restroom reconfigurations, widened openings, regraded routes. The engineer designs and seals the construction scope; legal compliance determinations belong to the architect, CASp inspector, or counsel, not the PE.",
