    h1: "Does a Historic Building Renovation Need PE Review?",
    answer: "A historic building renovation needs PE review whenever the work touches structure or MEP systems — which, in a building that's stood for a century, demands more care than new construction, not less. The engineer evaluates what the original builders actually constructed, often with undocumented modifications layered over decades, then designs upgrades that meet today's code without destroying the historic fabric the project exists to preserve. The AHJ reviews the sealed documents against the existing-building provisions, and the historic commission reviews the visible work against preservation standards — the PE's engineering has to satisfy both.\n\nWhat I do starts with investigation. Historic structures rarely match their drawings — if drawings exist at all. I field-verify the structural system: the actual framing, masonry, or early concrete construction; the condition after decades of service; and the undocumented alterations — removed walls, added openings, sistered members — that changed the load paths. Material testing sometimes enters: masonry prism tests, concrete cores, or wood species and grade identification where the capacity analysis needs real numbers instead of conservative assumptions. The evaluation documents the building as it stands, honestly including the deterioration and the surprises.\n\nThe upgrade design is where judgment matters most. The code's existing-building provisions offer compliance paths short of full new-construction standards, and I apply them deliberately — strengthening where life safety demands it, preserving where the provisions allow it. Seismic retrofits in California's historic building stock are the classic case: the engineering adds the lateral capacity the unreinforced masonry or non-ductile concrete lacks, detailed to minimize impact on historic finishes. MEP upgrades thread new systems through old construction with minimal disruption. The sealed set tells the AHJ the building is safe and tells the preservation review the building is still itself.",
