        heading: "Investigating the actual building",
        body: "The investigation phase is the historic PE review's foundation, and I budget it seriously — the engineering that follows is only as good as the understanding of the building. I start with the documentary record: historic drawings, Sanborn maps, prior alteration permits, and any earlier engineering reports, treating each as a lead rather than a fact. Then the field work: selective demolition openings at representative locations to expose the real framing, masonry, and connections; systematic documentation of conditions — deterioration mapped, not just noted; and a hunt for the undocumented modifications that every old building carries, because those modifications, not the original design, usually govern the structural behavior.\n\nMaterial investigation follows where the analysis requires it. Masonry shear testing, concrete compressive strength from cores, timber species and grade identification — each converts a conservative assumption into a known value, and known values let the upgrade design be surgical rather than wholesale. The investigation concludes with the structural evaluation report: the building's systems described as they are, capacities assessed, deficiencies identified, and the upgrade scope the code analysis will require. That report, sealed, is the document the whole renovation's engineering stands on.",
