    h1: "How Does PE Stamping Work for Phased Construction?",
    answer: "PE stamping for phased construction works as a series of partial seals: the engineer reviews and seals each phase's documents for that phase's permit, with the phases coordinated so the whole project converges on one coherent design. The PE defines the phase boundaries in engineering terms — what structure, what systems, and what interfaces each phase includes — seals the phase's drawings and calculations, and documents the assumptions about future phases that the current phase relies on. Each seal covers its phase completely; none covers the whole project until the final phase is sealed.\n\nWhat I do differently on phased work is manage the interfaces. Phase 1's foundation design has to anticipate Phase 2's loads; Phase 1's MEP rough-in has to serve Phase 2's fixtures; the lateral system built in Phase 1 has to stand alone safely until Phase 2 completes it. I define these interface requirements explicitly in each phase's sealed documents — the loads Phase 2 will impose, the connections Phase 2 will make, the temporary conditions Phase 1 must survive. Vague phase boundaries are where phased projects fail: the contractor builds Phase 1, Phase 2's design discovers Phase 1 can't accept it, and the rework costs dwarf the phasing savings.\n\nEOR coordination across phases is the ongoing discipline. The same engineer of record should carry all phases — splitting phases among different EORs multiplies the interface risk — and each phase's review verifies consistency with the prior phases' sealed documents and the project's overall design criteria. As-built verification between phases catches the drift: if Phase 1's construction deviated from its sealed drawings, Phase 2's design has to know before it relies on Phase 1. When each phase is sealed against explicit interfaces and verified predecessors, phased construction delivers its schedule advantages without its rework penalties.",
