    title: "What Is a Deferred Submittal and Who Seals It?",
    description: "Deferred submittals like trusses, curtain wall, and sprinklers are engineered after the main permit, sealed by the specialty PE, and reviewed by the EOR.",
    h1: "What Is a Deferred Submittal and Who Seals It?",
    answer: "A deferred submittal is a portion of the project the code allows to be engineered after the main building permit issues — typically specialty systems like roof trusses, curtain wall, fire sprinklers, precast concrete, or stairs — and it's sealed by the specialty engineer who designs it, then reviewed and approved by the engineer of record before installation. The deferral isn't a loophole; it's a formal permit mechanism: the main permit's documents identify the deferred items, state the design criteria they must meet, and the deferred engineering arrives later as its own sealed submittal. Nothing deferred gets installed until the EOR reviews and the AHJ approves it.\n\nThe responsibility chain has three links and I keep them explicit. The EOR specifies the criteria: the loads, the performance requirements, the code edition, and the interface conditions the deferred system must satisfy. The specialty engineer — a PE licensed in the project's state — designs the system to those criteria under their own responsible charge and seals the deferred submittal drawings and calculations. Then the EOR reviews the submittal for conformance with the specified criteria and the project's overall design, approves or returns it with corrections, and the AHJ gives the final approval. Each link owns its scope; no link borrows another's.\n\nWhat goes wrong is treating deferral as an afterthought. Contractors sometimes procure deferred systems — ordering the curtain wall, fabricating the trusses — before the EOR review, betting the submittal will pass unchanged. When it doesn't, the fabricated system needs expensive modification or the EOR faces pressure to approve a non-conforming design. My rule on projects I seal: no procurement of deferred systems before EOR submittal approval, stated in the project requirements. The deferred submittal review typically takes days when the package is complete — there is no schedule justification for skipping it.",
