    h1: "What Is a Final Inspection PE Certification Letter?",
    answer: "A final inspection PE certification letter is the engineer of record's professional statement, issued at project closeout, that the engineered work was constructed in substantial conformance with the sealed drawings — and it's often the document the AHJ requires before issuing the temporary or final certificate of occupancy. The letter closes the loop the PE's seal opened: the seal certified the design, the certification letter confirms the construction executed it. It's issued after the EOR's final site review, with the special inspection records and observation reports assembled behind it.\n\nWhat I verify before writing it is the complete field record. I conduct the final structural observation visit — walking the completed work against the sealed drawings, confirming the lateral system, the gravity framing, and the critical connections are as designed. I review the special inspection reports for the project: every required inspection performed, every reported deficiency dispositioned with the engineering rationale documented. Deferred submittals get confirmed: each approved, each installed per the approved submittal. The MEP side gets the same treatment where I sealed it: equipment installed per the schedules, systems tested and balanced, the installations matching the sealed design.\n\nThe letter states its basis plainly: the site visits performed and their dates, the inspection records reviewed, and the EOR's conclusion of substantial conformance — noting any accepted deviations with their engineering justification. Where the field record has gaps — a missing special inspection report, an unobserved milestone — the letter states the gap and how it was resolved, or the EOR withholds the letter until it's closed. AHJs accept these letters because they rest on a documented record; a closeout letter without the record behind it is a signature masquerading as an engineering certification, and no competent EOR issues one.",
