        heading: "Building the closeout record",
        body: "The closeout record is built during construction, not at the end of it. From the first structural observation visit, I file each report; as special inspection reports arrive, I log them and disposition deficiencies in writing with the engineering rationale; approved deferred submittals go in the record with their EOR review letters; material certifications and test reports accumulate in the file. By the time construction nears completion, the record is substantially assembled — the final site review then verifies the record against the finished building rather than reconstructing it from memory.\n\nGaps get closed before the letter, not papered over in it. A missing special inspection report triggers a records search first, then — if truly missing — an engineering evaluation of whether the uninspected work can be verified by other means: exposed for re-inspection, tested, or analyzed from the as-built condition. An unobserved milestone gets a targeted site visit. The principle is that the certification letter's every statement traces to evidence, and where evidence can't be produced, the letter says so and states the engineering basis for the conclusion anyway — or the conclusion is withheld. That discipline is what separates a professional certification from a signature.",
